# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
import json, io

data = {
 "pdf_page": 409,
 "book_page": 379,
 "verified_by": "claude-opus-5",
 "verified_on": "2026-09-13",
 "entries": [
  {
   "entry": 0,
   "root": "Pulâka",
   "verdict": "confirmed",
   "modern_form": "Sanskrit पुलाक (pulāka) m. 'a lump/ball of boiled rice'; a second homonym means 'shrivelled, blighted grain'",
   "note": "The root box is right in substance: Sanskrit pulāka 'a lump of boiled rice' is the accepted ultimate source of the pilaf word-family. Nourai's own citation KLN:1185 does not in fact reach Sanskrit — Klein's entry stops at 'Turk. pilaw, fr. Pers. palav' — but Hobson-Jobson p.710, which Nourai also cites on the Persian node, states the root verbatim: 'Pers. pulāo, or pilāv, Skt. pulāka, \"a ball of boiled rice.\"' Modern lexicography (Wiktionary, after Turner/CDIAL) adds that Sanskrit pulāka is itself probably a Dravidian loan (cf. Tamil puḻukku / puṟukkal 'cooked rice') and that the word reached Persian through Middle Indo-Aryan (Śaurasenī Prakrit *pulāga) and Hindustani पुलाव / پلاؤ pulāv, rather than straight from Sanskrit.",
   "note_fa": "ریشهٔ سنسکریت «پُلاکَ» (pulāka) به معنی «گلولهٔ برنج پخته» واقعی است و همان سرچشمهٔ خانوادهٔ واژگانی پلو/pilaf به شمار می‌رود؛ هابسن‌جابسن (ص ۷۱۰) عیناً همین را می‌آورد. اما ارجاع خودِ نورایی، یعنی کلاین ص ۱۱۸۵، تا سنسکریت پیش نمی‌رود و تنها می‌گوید «ترکی pilaw از فارسی palav». ریشه‌شناسی امروزی دو نکته می‌افزاید: نخست آنکه pulāka در خود سنسکریت احتمالاً وام‌واژه‌ای دراویدی است (سنجیده با تامیلی puḻukku «برنج پخته»)، و دوم آنکه راه ورود آن به فارسی از میانِ زبان‌های هندی میانه و هندوستانی (پُلاو) گذشته است، نه مستقیم از سنسکریت. ادعای نورایی دربارهٔ ریشه درست است.",
   "sources": [
    "https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/%E0%A4%AA%E0%A5%81%E0%A4%B2%E0%A4%BE%E0%A4%95",
    "https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/pilaf"
   ],
   "ref_check": [
    {"ref": "KLN:1185", "status": "partial", "note": "KLN_1966.txt leaf 633 (running numbers 1184/1185), s.v. 'pilau, pilaf, pilaw, n., an Oriental dish of rice boiled with meat, etc. — Turk. pilaw, fr. Pers. palav.' The page is the right one, but Klein gives no Sanskrit etymon at all, so it does not support the root box itself."}
   ],
   "consulted": [
    {"src": "HJB", "where": "p. 710 s.v. PILAU, PILOW, PILAF", "stance": "supports", "note": "'Pers. pulāo, or pilāv, Skt. pulāka, \"a ball of boiled rice.\" A dish, in origin purely Mahommedan, consisting of meat, or fowl, boiled along with rice and spices.' (grep '^PILAU' HJB.txt, line 17494; the next page marker reads 'PINANG, 711')."},
    {"src": "KLN", "where": "p. 1185 s.v. pilau", "stance": "partial", "note": "only 'Turk. pilaw, fr. Pers. palav' — no Sanskrit."},
    {"src": "FSD", "where": "vol. III s.v. pilau", "stance": "partial", "note": "'pi-lau, n. A mixed dish of boiled rice, raisins, spice, and some kind of meat or fowl much used among Mohammedans in oriental countries. [< Turk. pilaw, < Per. pilāw]' (FSD_vol3.txt line 151977) — stops at Persian."}
   ],
   "nodes": [
    {
     "id": 1,
     "lang": "Persian",
     "words": "polow",
     "verdict": "plausible",
     "derivation": "NP پلو polow, older/literary پلاو pilāw ~ palāw and the clipped پلا pilā, 'rice boiled and finished in fat with meat'. The dish and its name are late — there is no Middle Persian antecedent, and Horn's Grundriss and Hübschmann's Persische Studien, which cover the inherited Iranian vocabulary, both pass it over in silence. Hobson-Jobson p.710 gives exactly Nourai's arrow ('Pers. pulāo, or pilāv, Skt. pulāka'), and the earliest recipes are Indo-Persian (Āʾīn-i Akbarī, c. 1590, has a būrāna pulāo). Modern work refines the route: Sanskrit pulāka → Śaurasenī Prakrit *pulāga → Hindi पुलाव pulāv / Urdu پلاؤ pulāo → Classical Persian پلاو pilāw, i.e. the word entered Persian from Indo-Aryan in the Timurid/Mughal period rather than directly out of Sanskrit; the shift -ā- > -o- in the modern clipped form polow is the regular NP majhūl/colloquial development of final -āw > -ow (cf. کاو > گاو type, تاو > تو). Semantically the Indian 'ball of boiled rice' widened to 'rice cooked with fat and meat'. Nourai's Sanskrit→Persian arrow is therefore compatible but skips the Indo-Aryan intermediary.",
